    {
      "id": "emergent_international_ai_refugee_crisis",
      "description": "As AI-driven job displacement accelerates in high-income sectors, skilled professionals from across Europe begin relocating en masse to jurisdictions with active AI integration and labour guarantees, creating a new category of 'AI refugee' — talent flight not from war or poverty, but from systemic obsolescence. Countries with sovereign AI infrastructure report sharp increases in skilled migration applications, while EU member states see growing departures of mid-career technologists and legal professionals.",
      "first_turn": 13,
      "last_turn": 13
    },
    {
      "id": "emergent_ai_hardware_black_market",
      "description": "With export controls tightening and compute access increasingly politicized, a black market in high-performance AI hardware emerges, operating through shell companies and grey-zone logistics networks. Used H100s, bypassed export tags, and smuggled chiplets begin circulating in Eastern Europe and the Balkans, enabling unregulated AI development outside state oversight. Law enforcement agencies report seizures, but the trade proves resilient due to high demand and fragmented jurisdictional response.",
      "first_turn": 13,
      "last_turn": 13
    }
  ],
